<%!--
The layout every LiveView in `live_session :default` is wrapped in, and the
only reason it exists: it is the one place a *sticky* nested LiveView can be
rendered from (bd-dlc136).

`live_render/3` needs a `%Phoenix.LiveView.Socket{}`, and the root layout does
not have one — it is rendered by `Phoenix.Controller` off the conn, so `@socket`
there is nil. This layout is rendered by the LiveView process itself
(`Phoenix.LiveView.Renderer`), which passes `:socket` in the render assigns, so
`live_render(@socket, ..., sticky: true)` works here and nowhere else in the
chrome.

Being re-rendered per LiveView is exactly what `sticky: true` is for: on a
`live_redirect` the client moves the existing dock element into the incoming
main container and diffs the new markup against it, so the dock's process, DOM
node, hooks and scroll position all survive the navigation instead of being
re-mounted.

`#session-dock-offset` is the page's bottom breathing room. The strip is
`position: fixed`, so without it the last rows of a long page would sit
underneath the dock.

`loopback?` is handed down rather than read by the dock: `get_connect_info/2`
is root-and-mount only, and the dock is a nested child. It decides whether the
expanded window mounts a terminal at all (§10.4 / bd-2zskbb) — off loopback the
`/session` socket refuses a browser that has no token to send.
--%>
<div id="session-dock-offset" class="pb-[var(--session-dock-strip-height)]">
  {@inner_content}
</div>
{live_render(@socket, ArbiterWeb.SessionDockLive,
  id: "session-dock",
  sticky: true,
  session: %{"loopback?" => @loopback?}
)}
